{"title":"Alder Grand Hotel","sub":"How many extra bookings should a full hotel take?","type":"Pricing","industry":"Hospitality","geo":"United States","diff":3,"fmt":"Candidate-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Overbooking · expected value · probability","prompt":"The Alder Grand is a 300-room conference hotel in Washington, DC. On busy weeknights it sells out, but about 8% of guests with bookings don't show up, leaving rooms empty that could have been sold. The revenue manager wants to accept extra bookings beyond 300. The general manager worries about turning away guests who do arrive. How many extra bookings should the hotel take?","clarify":[{"q":"Room economics?","a":"Rate about $250 a night; about $45 of costs per occupied room."},{"q":"No-shows?","a":"On busy weeknights, about 8% of booked guests don't arrive, independently of each other."},{"q":"Walking a guest?","a":"If more guests arrive than there are rooms, the hotel pays for a room at a nearby hotel plus transport and a goodwill voucher: about $400 per guest, plus reputational damage."},{"q":"Cancellation policy?","a":"No-shows aren't charged on most corporate rates."}],"structure":{"root":"How many bookings beyond 300 should the Alder Grand accept?","branches":[{"head":"Benefit of an extra booking","items":["Fills a no-show room: $205 margin"]},{"head":"Cost of an extra booking","items":["Walking a guest: $400"]},{"head":"Probability","items":["How many no-shows on a given night"]},{"head":"Other factors","items":["Reputation","Who gets walked"]}]},"hypothesis":"With 8% no-shows, about 24 rooms go empty on a sold-out night, but the number varies. Since walking a guest costs more than a room earns, the hotel should stop a little short of the bookings that would, on average, exactly fill the hotel.","questions":[{"q":"A simple answer would be to overbook by the expected number of no-shows. What is that, and what's wrong with it?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>To expect exactly 300 arrivals, book 300 ÷ 92% ≈ <b>326</b>, i.e. 26 extra. But arrivals vary from night to night, so on about half of those nights more than 300 guests would turn up.</p> <p>An extra booking that fills an empty room earns $205; one that arrives to a full hotel costs $400. Because the downside is bigger than the upside, the hotel should stop a little short of 26.</p>"},{"q":"Here is expected profit per night for different numbers of extra bookings. What do you recommend?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"394.408156pt\" height=\"170.908pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 394.408156 170.908\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt}</style></defs><g id=\"figure_1\"><g id=\"patch_1\"><path d=\"M -0 170.908 L 394.408156 170.908 L 394.408156 0 L -0 0 L -0 170.908 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"axes_1\"><g id=\"patch_2\"><path d=\"M 41.248156 145.8 L 387.208156 145.8 L 387.208156 7.2 L 41.248156 7.2 L 41.248156 145.8 z\n\" style=\"fill: none\"/></g><g id=\"matplotlib.axis_1\"><g id=\"xtick_1\"><g id=\"line2d_1\"><defs><path id=\"mff7d576334\" d=\"M 0 0 L 0 3.5 \" style=\"stroke: #555555; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></defs><g><use xlink:href=\"#mff7d576334\" x=\"56.973611\" y=\"145.8\" style=\"fill: #555555; stroke: #555555; stroke-width: 0.8\"/></g></g><g id=\"text_1\"><g style=\"fill: #555555\" transform=\"translate(53.877751 161.494) scale(0.09 -0.09)\"><defs><path id=\"LibreFranklin-Regular-80\" d=\"M 2208 4813 Q 2586 4813 2909 4656 Q 3232 4499 3472 4185 Q 3712 3872 3846 3414 Q 3981 2957 3981 2362 Q 3981 1568 3747 1030 Q 3514 493 3110 217 Q 2707 -58 2202 -58 Q 1690 -58 1286 217 Q 883 493 652 1030 Q 422 1568 422 2355 Q 422 2950 556 3408 Q 691 3866 934 4182 Q 1178 4499 1504 4656 Q 1830 4813 2208 4813 L 2208 4813 z\nM 2208 410 Q 2560 410 2816 608 Q 3072 806 3213 1238 Q 3354 1670 3354 2362 Q 3354 3066 3210 3501 Q 3066 3936 2806 4141 Q 2547 4346 2202 4346 Q 1856 4346 1597 